cdjot-specific tests: bugs found via corpus comparison against JS djot,
plus regression tests for cdjot bugs and performance fixes. Cases that
upstream has since adopted are covered by the upstream test files.

Inline attributes on links: {key=val}, {.class}, {#id} after
[text](url) should attach to the <a> element.

```
[a](b){rel=me}
.
<p><a href="b" rel="me">a</a></p>
```

```
[a](b){.cls #id}
.
<p><a href="b" class="cls" id="id">a</a></p>
```

A quoted value may contain `}`. dolink scans for the closing brace
itself rather than calling parse_attrs to find it, so that scan has to
skip quoted runs; ending it at the first `}` truncates the value and
leaves the rest as text.

```
[a](b){k="}"}
.
<p><a href="b" k="}">a</a></p>
```

Attributes are emitted in declaration order, and a repeated name keeps
its original position while taking the later value. Only classes
accumulate; a second id replaces the first rather than joining it.
attributes.test:145 asserts the same rule across consecutive blocks,
which cdjot merges in attr_merge; this is the within-one-spec path in
parse_attrs, and it is the only case covering it.

```
{#i1 .c key=a #i2}
para
.
<p id="i2" class="c" key="a">para</p>
```

Inline attributes on strong should attach to the element,
not wrap in a <span>.

```
*strong*{.a}
.
<p><strong class="a">strong</strong></p>
```

Inline attributes on sub/superscript:

```
~sub~{.a}
.
<p><sub class="a">sub</sub></p>
```

```
^sup^{.a}
.
<p><sup class="a">sup</sup></p>
```

Inline attributes on insert/delete:

```
{+ins+}{.a}
.
<p><ins class="a">ins</ins></p>
```

```
{-del-}{.a}
.
<p><del class="a">del</del></p>
```

Inline attributes on code spans, with a quoted value:

```
`code`{key="value"}
.
<p><code key="value">code</code></p>
```

Inline attributes on autolinks, with id and class:

```
<https://example.com>{#id .cls}
.
<p><a href="https://example.com" id="id" class="cls">https://example.com</a></p>
```

Combined inline attributes with id, class, and key=val:

```
_emph_{#id .cls key=val}
.
<p><em id="id" class="cls" key="val">emph</em></p>
```

An attribute block ahead of a reference definition applies to links
that use it — all of it, id and class included, not just key=value
pairs.

```
{#i .c key=v}
[foo]: http://x

[foo][]
.
<p><a href="http://x" id="i" class="c" key="v">foo</a></p>
```

A footnote reference inside a list item must remain valid after the
list ends, because list items are processed in a temporary recursion
buffer that is freed when the list returns. A later reference to the
same undefined label must still resolve to the same footnote.

```
- [^a]

then [^a]
.
<ul>
<li>
<a id="fnref1" href="#fn1" role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
</ul>
<p>then <a href="#fn1" role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a></p>
<section role="doc-endnotes">
<hr>
<ol>
<li id="fn1">
<p><a href="#fnref1" role="doc-backlink">↩︎</a></p>
</li>
</ol>
</section>
```

Identifier and class names accept only ASCII alphanumerics, `_`, `:`,
and `-` (matching the spec's rule for unquoted key=value values, and
matching djot.js). Any other character invalidates the entire
attribute spec, which then renders as literal text. This prevents
characters like `"` from being smuggled into HTML attribute contexts.
(attributes.test covers the `<` case.)

```
{.x"y}
foo
.
<p>{.x“y}
foo</p>
```

Quoted values are not restricted that way, so they are the one place
`<` and `&` can reach an attribute value. They must be HTML-escaped on
output so they can't break out of the attribute.

```
{k1="a<b" k2="c&d"}
para
.
<p k1="a&lt;b" k2="c&amp;d">para</p>
```

Inline attribute syntax with invalid chars renders the braces as
literal trailing text rather than silently consuming them: trailing
attrs on links, images, autolinks, and reference links are not
consumed. (For spans, the brackets go literal too — see spans.test.)

```
[link](url){.x"y}
.
<p><a href="url">link</a>{.x“y}</p>
```

Smart double quotes: djot.js puts no opener restriction on `"` -- any
`"` may open -- and renders every unmatched one as a left quote. A
closer takes the nearest pending opener, so a `"` after a completed pair
opens rather than closing anything. cdjot used to choose by looking at
the neighbouring characters, which made the third quote a right one.

smart.test's unmatched-`"` cases (:71, :214) both sit at a word
boundary, where the old rule opened correctly too, so they do not cover
this. The bug needs a non-space, non-punctuation character on both
sides, which is what the third quote here has.

```
a"b"c"d
.
<p>a“b”c“d</p>
```

Nesting needs the whole stack, not just the nearest opener. The inner
`"` here has a space before it so it cannot close, and opens a second
pair; the last `"` then has to reach past it to the first. smart.test:50
pins the same rule for `'` (`'tis the season to be 'jolly'`); upstream
has no `"` equivalent, so this is an upstream candidate too.

```
"a "b" c"
.
<p>“a “b” c”</p>
```

A quote pair is an inline container, so syntax.md's precedence rule
applies to it: once the pair closes, the `_` it encloses is literal and
can no longer open. cdjot used to substitute quote glyphs in doreplace,
outside the delimiter matching entirely, so the `_`s paired across the
closing quote and produced an <em> djot.js never emits.

```
"_"_
.
<p>“_”_</p>
```

A closer adjacent to the opener it would claim encloses nothing, so it
is refused -- and it then opens instead, rather than falling through to
an opener further out. Every quote in a run is therefore an opener.

```
""""
.
<p>““““</p>
```

Marked as both opener and closer, `{"}` matches as an opener, leaving
the `}` literal, but the glyph still flips once away from the kind's
default. So the double quote comes out right and the single one left.

```
{"}

{'}
.
<p>”}</p>
<p>‘}</p>
```

The blocks below came out of fixing quadratic scans. They assert output,
not running time -- a reintroduced O(N^2) would pass every one of them
-- so what they actually lock is the behaviour each fast path has to
preserve: a cache or short-circuit must not change what gets emitted.
The complexity notes record why the code is shaped the way it is.

A run of `^` with no possible closer falls through to a literal emit.
dosurround used to re-scan the whole run via an all-delim walk on every
call, O(N^3). (emphasis.test covers the `_` runs.)

```
^^^^^^^^^^
.
<p>^^^^^^^^^^</p>
```

Consecutive indented `[label]:` lines each register their own ref.
prescan used to re-walk continuations on every iteration, O(N^2), and
merged sibling refdefs into one URL.

```
  [a]: http://aa
  [b]: http://bb
[link][b]
.
<p><a href="http://bb">link</a></p>
```

A `[` with no matching `]` is skipped and the scan continues past it,
so the `^` here still pairs with the one on the next line. The bracket
is not what allows that -- `a^x` / `a^y` pairs identically -- so what
this pins is the skip itself: dosurround takes the match from the
bracket-match table to avoid walking to e on every `[`, O(N^2), and that
lookup has to continue past an unmatched `[`, not abandon the run.

```
[^x
[^y
.
<p>[<sup>x
[</sup>y</p>
```

A run of `'` openers with no closer anywhere stays apostrophes. Each
opener used to simulate stack matching to e, O(N^2) -- 200k of these
took 43s. Once a scan reaches e having seen no eligible closer, the
sq_nc_b cache tells the later openers not to look.

```
'a 'a 'a
.
<p>’a ’a ’a</p>
```

`{X` with X non-alpha and no closing `}` stays literal. doreplace used
to re-scan to the next `\n` on every `{`, O(N^2); the nc_b/nc_eol cache
makes it linear.

```
{1 {1 {1 {1 {1 {1 {1
.
<p>{1 {1 {1 {1 {1 {1 {1</p>
```

URL safety: a literal `<` or `&` in a destination must be HTML-escaped
in the emitted href/src so it can't break out of the attribute. Found
via fuzz/proptest attrsafe on AFL-mutated URLs. The inline link, the
inline image, the two reference paths, and the autolink each call the
shared emitter from their own site, and an emitter that skips the
escaping passes every upstream file at all five, so pin each one.

```
[link](/x?a=1&b=2<z)
.
<p><a href="/x?a=1&amp;b=2&lt;z">link</a></p>
```

```
![alt](/img/<svg)
.
<p><img alt="alt" src="/img/&lt;svg"></p>
```

```
[link][r]

[r]: /x?a=1&b=2<z
.
<p><a href="/x?a=1&amp;b=2&lt;z">link</a></p>
```

```
![alt][r]

[r]: /x?a=1&b=2<z
.
<p><img alt="alt" src="/x?a=1&amp;b=2&lt;z"></p>
```

Alt text lands inside a quoted HTML attribute, so a `"` in it must be
escaped too, or it closes the attribute and the rest becomes markup.
Body text leaves `"` alone, so this is the one place it is escaped. All
three <img> emit sites share the escaper; the reference one is pinned
here because it is reached separately.

```
![a" onerror=x][r]

[r]: u.png
.
<p><img alt="a&quot; onerror=x" src="u.png"></p>
```

An autolink cannot contain a literal `<` -- that would end it -- but an
`&` must still be escaped, in the href and in the link text alike:

```
<https://x.com/?a=1&b=2>
.
<p><a href="https://x.com/?a=1&amp;b=2">https://x.com/?a=1&amp;b=2</a></p>
```

A bare `key` or an empty `key=` is not valid attribute syntax: the spec
allows only `.class`, `#id`, `key=value`, `key="value"` and `%comment%`.
An unparseable `{...}` makes the whole construct literal text, including
the surrounding brackets (jgm/djot#399), so no attribute in the run is
applied and a failed block attribute line starts a paragraph.

```
[x]{key}

[y]{key=}

{.a key}
# not a heading
.
<p>[x]{key}</p>
<p>[y]{key=}</p>
<p>{.a key}
# not a heading</p>
```

Headings used to emit only id and class, silently dropping any other
key=value attribute. All attributes are emitted, in declaration order.
The id is the exception: it goes on the enclosing <section>, and only
when a heading is inside a container (so no section is opened) does it
appear on the tag itself -- prepended when auto-generated, in place when
written explicitly.

```
{a=b .c}
# abc

:::
{a=b #x}
## sub
:::
.
<section id="abc">
<h1 a="b" class="c">abc</h1>
<div>
<h2 a="b" id="x">sub</h2>
</div>
</section>
```

That rule holds whether the id is auto-generated or written out. djot.js
agrees on the auto-generated case above, but moves the whole attribute
set onto the <section> when the id is explicit, leaving the heading bare
-- so this one case diverges. djot-php matches cdjot. Which behaviour is
right is open upstream as jgm/djot.js#144.

```
{a=b .c #x}
# abc
.
<section id="x">
<h1 a="b" class="c">abc</h1>
</section>
```
